WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:05.440
Picture a friend who is good in the kitchen.

2
00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:09.131
You hand them any recipe card, and they hand back a new one.

3
00:00:09.131 --> 00:00:10.915
The same, but for six people.

4
00:00:10.915 --> 00:00:12.145
The same, but vegan.

5
00:00:12.145 --> 00:00:14.421
The same, and if it burns, try again.

6
00:00:14.421 --> 00:00:17.251
They don't need to know what the recipe makes.

7
00:00:17.251 --> 00:00:19.527
They change the card, not the dinner.

8
00:00:19.667 --> 00:00:22.583
Here's a question that's easy to be shy about.

9
00:00:22.583 --> 00:00:26.260
A method's parameters are values: numbers, strings, lists.

10
00:00:26.260 --> 00:00:28.352
How do you pass in a thing to do?

11
00:00:28.352 --> 00:00:30.317
And why would you ever want to?

12
00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:35.252
Look at three places in a recipe app that call something flaky.

13
00:00:35.252 --> 00:00:42.712
A flaky call fails now and then, for reasons outside your code, like a website that is slow to answer and times out.

14
00:00:42.712 --> 00:00:48.436
So each place wraps its call in the same retry loop: try up to three times, then give up.

15
00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:51.630
The three loops are the same, except for one line.

16
00:00:51.630 --> 00:00:53.448
The first copy was convenient.

17
00:00:53.448 --> 00:00:54.720
The third is a habit.

18
00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:59.144
And when someone finds a bug in it, they'll fix exactly one of the three.

19
00:01:00.067 --> 00:01:05.073
Wouldn't it be nice to write the loop once, and pass in the one line that changes?

20
00:01:05.073 --> 00:01:09.957
Here's the obvious try: a retry method that takes the fetch as a plain argument.

21
00:01:10.133 --> 00:01:11.298
Look at that call.

22
00:01:11.298 --> 00:01:13.432
How many times can the fetch run?

23
00:01:16.467 --> 00:01:18.780
Once, and before retry even starts.

24
00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:23.936
An argument is a value, so the fetch runs first, and retry is handed the page.

25
00:01:23.936 --> 00:01:27.505
If that one call times out, retry never gets a chance.

26
00:01:27.505 --> 00:01:31.405
What we need to pass in is the work itself, not its answer.

27
00:01:32.333 --> 00:01:34.348
That's the friend from the start.

28
00:01:34.348 --> 00:01:36.242
You hand over a card: the work.

29
00:01:36.242 --> 00:01:39.417
The friend decides when to follow it, and how often.

30
00:01:39.417 --> 00:01:45.096
And the friend never needs to know what the card makes: a page, a price list, or the weather.

31
00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:52.257
A function that takes a function as a parameter, or returns one, is called a higher-order function.

32
00:01:52.257 --> 00:01:54.027
Here's retry written as one.

33
00:01:54.027 --> 00:01:58.641
The work arrives as block, a function that takes nothing and returns a T.

34
00:01:58.641 --> 00:02:03.191
The loop calls block up to times times, and hands back the first answer.

35
00:02:03.191 --> 00:02:07.047
If every try fails, it gives up with the last error attached.

36
00:02:07.933 --> 00:02:12.951
Java needs an interface for the parameter, here a Supplier, and calls it with get.

37
00:02:12.951 --> 00:02:17.419
Kotlin has a real function type, and calls block like any other function.

38
00:02:18.333 --> 00:02:23.179
The lesson's demo has a fake website that times out twice, and then answers.

39
00:02:23.179 --> 00:02:26.240
With retry three, how many calls reach the site?

40
00:02:29.267 --> 00:02:29.697
Three.

41
00:02:29.697 --> 00:02:31.705
Two timeouts, then the page.

42
00:02:31.705 --> 00:02:35.793
And neither retry nor the website knows what a recipe is.

43
00:02:35.933 --> 00:02:42.325
The demo wraps it in a second friend, timed, which runs any block and reports how long it took.

44
00:02:42.325 --> 00:02:45.150
A friend around a friend, around the card.

45
00:02:45.150 --> 00:02:48.245
It printed 41 milliseconds, on a busy machine.

46
00:02:48.245 --> 00:02:52.685
Most of that is the JVM running brand new code for the first time.

47
00:02:53.600 --> 00:02:56.433
Now a site that stays down for five calls.

48
00:02:56.433 --> 00:03:05.133
retry tries three times, and gives up with a clear message: gave up after 3 attempts, and the last cause, site timeout on call 3.

49
00:03:05.333 --> 00:03:09.331
Change it to retry one, and it says: gave up after 1 attempts.

50
00:03:09.331 --> 00:03:12.426
That message never expected to be used this way.

51
00:03:12.426 --> 00:03:16.553
A little bad grammar is a small price for writing the loop once.

52
00:03:17.467 --> 00:03:20.229
One more thing makes this pleasant in Kotlin.

53
00:03:20.229 --> 00:03:25.508
When the last parameter is a function, you can write the lambda after the parentheses.

54
00:03:25.508 --> 00:03:27.472
That's called a trailing lambda.

55
00:03:27.472 --> 00:03:31.830
So the call reads like a keyword: retry three, then the work in braces.

56
00:03:31.830 --> 00:03:34.961
That's why repeat, use and buildList look built in.

57
00:03:34.961 --> 00:03:37.539
They're ordinary functions in a nice coat.

58
00:03:38.467 --> 00:03:46.105
Java has one more hurdle: checked exceptions, the ones the compiler makes you catch or declare, like IOException.

59
00:03:46.105 --> 00:03:53.676
Supplier's get can't throw one, so retrying file or network code needs a Callable, and a throws clause on retry.

60
00:03:53.676 --> 00:03:58.476
Kotlin has no checked exceptions, so one function type fits every call.

61
00:03:59.400 --> 00:04:01.379
Here the friend picture breaks.

62
00:04:01.379 --> 00:04:06.358
A real friend would read the card, and notice when trying again is a bad idea.

63
00:04:06.358 --> 00:04:07.699
retry never reads it.

64
00:04:07.699 --> 00:04:10.763
It runs whatever you hand it, up to three times.

65
00:04:10.763 --> 00:04:14.338
Remember the fridge from the first lesson of this track?

66
00:04:14.338 --> 00:04:18.998
A call that takes an egg, retried after a failure, may take a second egg.

67
00:04:18.998 --> 00:04:23.020
Retry is safe for a call that only reads, like fetching a page.

68
00:04:23.933 --> 00:04:29.284
So the rule: when code differs by one call, pass the call in, and write the loop once.

69
00:04:29.284 --> 00:04:34.635
Keep the shape of the work, like retrying it or timing it, apart from the work itself.

70
00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:37.988
And the friend can also hand back a new card.

71
00:04:37.988 --> 00:04:47.764
Ask gramsFor for honey, and it hands back a converter, from the measuring jug to the kitchen scale: 250 millilitres of honey is 355 grams.

72
00:04:47.764 --> 00:04:50.244
That's the next part of the lesson.

73
00:04:51.133 --> 00:04:53.285
So, how do you pass in a thing to do?

74
00:04:53.285 --> 00:04:54.448
You pass a function.

75
00:04:54.448 --> 00:04:59.857
The friend holds the loop, you hold the work, and neither needs to know the other's business.

76
00:04:60.000 --> 00:05:00.840
Quick check.

77
00:05:00.840 --> 00:05:04.617
timed wraps retry, around a site that times out twice.

78
00:05:04.617 --> 00:05:06.996
How many timing lines get printed?

79
00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:10.285
One.

80
00:05:10.285 --> 00:05:14.984
timed wraps the whole retry, so it times all three calls together.
